      </div>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Step 2: Gather and apostille documents</h3>
      <p>Begin with the FBI background check (allow 8–14 weeks for standard FBI processing). Simultaneously gather income proof, medical certificate, and health insurance. Apostille all US-issued documents through the US Department of State.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Step 3: Get sworn Spanish translations</h3>
      <p>Every apostilled document must be translated into Spanish by a sworn translator (traductor jurado) recognized by Spain. Find accredited translators via the Spanish consulate website or the Spanish Ministry of Foreign Affairs directory.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Step 4: Book and attend your consulate appointment</h3>
      <p>Book an appointment via the consulate's online portal. Bring originals and certified copies of all documents. Pay the visa fee (approximately €80). Processing time after submission: 8–16 weeks.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Step 5: Collect visa and travel to Spain</h3>
      <p>Once approved, collect your visa sticker from the consulate. You must enter Spain within the visa's validity period (usually 90 days). After arrival, register at the local Extranjería office within 30 days to obtain your NIE/TIE residence card.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Tax Implications ──────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section--cream">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>US Tax Obligations: The Critical Issue for American Retirees</h2>
        <p>Unlike most nationalities, Americans have a unique tax obligation: the US taxes its citizens on worldwide income regardless of where they live. Moving to Spain does not end your US tax filing obligation.</p>
      </div>
      <p>As a US citizen living in Spain, you will be subject to taxation in both countries. Here's how it works:</p>
      <ul style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <li><strong>You must still file US taxes:</strong> US citizens living abroad must file Form 1040 every year. There is no exemption from filing simply because you live in Spain.</li>
        <li><strong>Spain taxes your worldwide income:</strong> As a Spanish tax resident (living in Spain more than 183 days per year), Spain's Agencia Tributaria taxes all your worldwide income — including US Social Security, pensions, and investment income.</li>
        <li><strong>Foreign Tax Credit (Form 1116):</strong> You can offset Spanish taxes paid against your US tax liability. This is the primary mechanism to avoid double taxation.</li>
        <li><strong>Foreign Earned Income Exclusion (FEIE):</strong> Note — the FEIE applies to earned income (wages, self-employment), NOT passive retirement income. It will not reduce US tax on Social Security or pension income.</li>
        <li><strong>FBAR and FATCA reporting:</strong> If you hold more than $10,000 in foreign bank accounts, you must file FinCEN Form 114 (FBAR). Spanish bank accounts must also be reported on Form 8938 (FATCA) if thresholds are met.</li>
        <li><strong>Social Security treaty treatment:</strong> The US-Spain tax treaty has a specific provision for Social Security. Under the treaty, US Social Security benefits paid to a Spanish resident are generally only taxable in the US — but professional advice is strongly recommended as individual circumstances vary.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--red">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">⚠️</span>
        <p><strong>Critical for Americans:</strong> US tax obligations as an expat are complex. Before moving to Spain, consult a CPA or tax attorney who specializes in US expat taxation and the US-Spain tax treaty. Failure to file can result in significant penalties. This is one area where professional advice is not optional.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

