        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions: Spain Retirement Visa for Americans</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="msv__faq-list">
        <details>
          <summary>What is the Spain retirement visa for US citizens called?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>The Spain retirement visa for US citizens is the Non-Lucrative Visa (NLV), officially called Visado de Residencia No Lucrativa. It allows Americans to live in Spain long-term without working, provided they have sufficient passive income — at least approximately €2,400/month for a single applicant in 2026. Social Security, IRA distributions, pension income, and investment income all count toward this threshold.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which US consulate handles my Spain NLV application?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>You must apply through the Spanish consulate with jurisdiction over your US state of legal residence. The six consulates are: New York (Northeast), Los Angeles (West Coast), Miami (Southeast + Florida), Chicago (Midwest), Houston (Texas and Southwest), and Washington DC (DC Metro area). Always apply at the consulate covering where you legally reside — not where you plan to fly from.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does US Social Security count as income for the Spain retirement visa?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. US Social Security retirement benefits count as qualifying passive income for the Spanish Non-Lucrative Visa. You'll need an official benefits verification letter from the Social Security Administration showing your monthly benefit amount. For 2026, a single applicant needs approximately €2,400/month (roughly $2,600 USD). IRA and 401k distributions, private pensions, rental income, and investment dividends also qualify.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What is the FBI background check requirement for Americans?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Americans must provide an FBI Identity History Summary — a federal-level background check. State-level police checks are not accepted by Spanish consulates. Apply via the FBI website or through an authorized channeler for faster processing (3–5 days vs. 8–14 weeks). After receiving it, the document must be apostilled by the US Department of State and then translated into Spanish by a sworn translator.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How does the US-Spain tax treaty affect American retirees in Spain?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>The US-Spain double taxation treaty helps prevent Americans from being taxed twice on the same income. However, the US is unusual in taxing citizens on worldwide income regardless of residence — so you must still file US tax returns even after moving to Spain. As a Spanish resident, you'll also file Spanish taxes. The Foreign Tax Credit (Form 1116) allows you to offset Spanish taxes paid against your US liability. Consult a CPA specializing in US expat taxation before moving.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

